# Invitation product

One Next.js app and one Vercel project. Templates are skins. A sale is content (names, date, photos) plus a template id — never a copied repo or a new Vercel app per client.

Keep event copy out of components; pass event data from `src/events/` (schema in `src/content/types.ts`). Shared blocks: hero, countdown, gallery, details, map, RSVP.

**Countdown is required on every skin.** Always render [`Countdown`](src/components/Countdown.tsx) from `event.iso` (look and placement may change; omitting it is not allowed). Other blocks may be restyled or reordered; do not fork the content schema without updating `docs/content-schema.md`.

One RSVP pipeline for all templates. Do not add a new email/sheet/db integration per skin.

Do not implement the full `docs/templates-catalog.md` list in one go. The work queue is **GitHub Issues** ([docs/backlog.md](docs/backlog.md)): only the open issue labeled `now`. After finishing, offer to close it and move `now`. Propose priority changes explicitly if a sales blocker is not the current `now`.

**Before any new work item:** do not create a GitHub issue, add labels, or start coding a new task until the user **explicitly agrees** to put it in the backlog (and with which priority). Propose the issue title/body/labels first; wait for yes. “Implement the plan” on an already-agreed `now` issue is fine — inventing or opening the next issue without consent is not.

Read `docs/product.md` for deploy/URL model and `AGENTS.md` for the same rules.

`main` is protected. Always branch from `origin/main` and open a PR. Prefix = why the PR exists: `feature/` (new behavior), `fix/` (broken), `docs/` (docs only), `chore/` (scripts/CI/env). Do not use `refactor/` or push to `main`. One `now` task = one branch; mixed docs+code stays there — not a second docs PR. Say the branch name before starting. Details: `docs/product.md` § Git branches.
